<Legal
    title="Privacy policy"
    description="What Sikemux collects, why, who handles it, and how to have it deleted."
    updated="26 November 2026">
    <p>
        Sikemux is a product of Nodelike. This page covers the Sikemux app for your computer, the Sikemux phone app, your
        Sikemux account at <a href="https://app.sikemux.com">app.sikemux.com</a>, and this website. Questions go to <a href="mailto:contact@nodelike.com">contact@nodelike.com</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            Your code, files, terminals, agent chats, prompts and dictation stay on your devices. We never receive them,
            unless you share them, and then sealed so we cannot read them.
        </li>
        <li>
            Connections between your devices are encrypted end to end. A relay server that passes them along cannot read
            them. Notifications from your computer to your phone are encrypted on your computer, with a key only your
            phone has.
        </li>
        <li>
            A Sikemux account holds your email address and the list of devices signed in to it. It exists so your
            devices can find each other.
        </li>
        <li>We do not sell data, show ads, or share data with anyone except the services listed below.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Every connection the app makes</h2>
    <p>
        Besides your coding agents, which each talk to their own company, these are all the places the Sikemux app for
        your computer connects to.
    </p>
    <div class="connections">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>When</th><th>Where</th><th>What it sends</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Daily totals and first-run steps, after first-run setup (at launch before 0.6.4), unless sharing is off</td><td>PostHog, EU</td><td>Daily totals and first-run steps, with a random install ID and a hash of your account if signed in, described below</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Crashes, sessions and some startup traces, after first-run setup (at launch before 0.6.4), unless sharing is off</td><td>Sentry, EU</td><td>Code addresses, image names and builds, recent warning and error logs (paths stripped), device and OS. Before 0.6.4, native crashes sent minidumps.</td></tr>
