    <h2>Your Sikemux account</h2>
    <p>
        The phone app needs an account. The app on your computer works without one and asks you to sign in only to be
        found by your phone. When you have an account, we keep:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Who you are:</strong> your email address, and the name and profile picture Google or GitHub share if
            you sign in with them. The Sikemux apps show them so you can see which account you are signed in to. If you choose a password, only a hash of it is kept, never the password itself.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Your devices:</strong> for each device signed in to your account, its name (such as your computer's
            name or your phone's model), its platform, which Sikemux release it runs, the public key it identifies itself
            with, and when it was added and last updated.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>A record of account changes:</strong> when a device was added to or removed from your account, so you
            and we can see what happened to it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Sign-in sessions:</strong> which browsers and devices are signed in, with the IP address and browser
            details they signed in from, to keep your account secure.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Notifications:</strong> if you turn them on, the address Apple or Google gives your phone for
            notifications, and which kind of phone it is. We delete it when you sign out, remove the phone, or turn
            notifications off.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Shared artifacts:</strong> an artifact you share is sealed on your computer with a key that is only
            in its link, so we keep it without being able to read it. Its title and your profile name are not sealed: we
            and the apps you post the link in can read them, to show the link's preview. We drop the artifact, its title
            and your name when you stop sharing, within an hour of the link expiring, or when you delete your account.
            An app that already showed a preview may keep its own copy.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Shared chats:</strong> a chat you share is sealed the same way and passed on to whoever has the link
            while it is open. Nothing of it is left unsealed, not even its title, and we keep none of it once the share
            ends.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Notifications from your computer to your phone are encrypted on your computer, with a key only your phone has. We
        pass them to Apple or Google to deliver without being able to read them; we and they see only that a notification
        went from one of your devices to another, when, and roughly how large it was.
    </p>
    <p>
        Signing out of the phone app removes that phone from your account. Signing out on your computer removes it from
        your account too; phones already paired with it keep working.
    </p>

    <h2>Server logs</h2>
    <p>
        Our servers log each request they handle: the address asked for, the answer, how long it took, and which account
        made it. The web server also logs the IP address and browser of each visit. We use these logs to keep the service
        running and to investigate abuse, and they are deleted automatically as newer logs replace them.
    </p>

    <h2>Usage statistics and crash reports</h2>
    <p>
        <strong>PostHog, EU (desktop only):</strong> official releases of the desktop app and headless host send one
        message a day while you use them, plus each first-run step once (setup started, an agent ready, first message,
        second day). Each message holds a random install ID, a hash of your Sikemux account if signed in, app version,
        update channel, OS, OS version, Linux distro and CPU type. The hash does not name you, but we can match it to
        your account. Daily totals hold the day before's counts: tokens and cost by agent and model, turns, chat and
        terminal sessions, commits, model calls, compactions, wait counts and how often each feature was used. None of
        this holds your files, prompts, commands, project names or code. From 0.6.4, nothing is sent until first-run
        setup finishes (or a headless host signs in). Before 0.6.4, reporting started at launch and could send earlier
        local daily totals.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Sentry, EU (desktop):</strong> official desktop releases send panics, native crashes, app, core and notch
        sessions, and 10% of startup timing traces. Recent warning and error log lines go along with file paths and your
        home folder stripped, plus the same account hash. From 0.6.4, Sentry follows the same rule as PostHog:
        nothing is sent until first-run setup finishes, unless sharing is off. Before 0.6.4, Sentry started at launch.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Native crash reports:</strong> from 0.6.4 on desktop and the Mac notch, and 0.2.2 on iPhone, native
        crashes send only the crash reason, the crashed thread's code addresses, image file names and build IDs, and
        device and OS details. No memory and no CPU registers leave the machine. Desktop releases before 0.6.4 sent
        minidumps, and notch and iPhone releases before 0.6.4 and 0.2.2 did not strip registers or disable memory
        introspection. Android still sends CPU registers in native crash reports.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Phones and app.sikemux.com:</strong> official phone builds send crashes, errors and sessions to Sentry
        EU (iPhone also sends a report when the app hangs for 2 seconds or more, and Android 5% of startup and
        performance traces), with app version, device and OS details and the same account hash, which we can match to
        your account. Neither phone app sends PostHog usage statistics. The web app at app.sikemux.com sends
        errors and 5% of page-load traces to Sentry EU, with URL queries and fragments stripped and the same account hash
        when signed in. Both phone apps and the web app can send before you have opened their Account screen to see
        the switch. Turn off <strong>Share crash reports</strong> in the phone's <strong>Account</strong> sheet (on
        Android from 0.2.2; earlier Android builds had no switch) or on the <strong>Account</strong> page at
        app.sikemux.com. The web app's switch is stored in that browser and follows only its own switch; turning sharing
        off on desktop or phone does not turn it off on the web.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Turning it off on desktop:</strong> new installs from 0.6.4 show the sharing switch during first-run
        setup, or a notice with <strong>Turn off sharing</strong> when setup finishes automatically. If you set up
        Sikemux earlier, you never saw that setup switch; change it any time in <strong>Settings &rarr; About &rarr;
        Privacy</strong>. From 0.6.4, turning it off stops PostHog and Sentry at once, and days spent off are skipped so
        turning it back on never sends them later. Before 0.6.4, Sentry kept sending for the rest of that run until
        restart. Setting <code>DO_NOT_TRACK</code> (or <code>CI</code>) in the environment turns off all desktop PostHog
        and Sentry reporting: from 0.6.4, any value except empty, <code>0</code>, <code>false</code>, <code>no</code> or
        <code>off</code> (case-insensitive) counts as on; before 0.6.4, only empty, <code>0</code> and <code>false</code>
        were treated as off.
    </p>
    <p>
        PostHog and Sentry see the sender's IP address in transit, as any server does; Sikemux tells Sentry not to store
        or geolocate IP addresses. Builds you make yourself never send any of this.
    </p>
    <p>
        This website counts page views and clicks on the Download button with PostHog EU, without cookies and without
        identifying you.
    </p>

    <h2>What the apps download</h2>
    <p>
        The desktop app checks GitHub for updates and downloads them from there, along with the profile pictures of the
        people credited in its release notes. It refreshes model prices, context sizes and capabilities from models.dev
        at most once a day when the bundled copy is stale. If you use dictation, it downloads the speech model from
        Hugging Face once, and then turns speech into text on your computer. If you press <strong>Set up</strong> to
        install the Android emulator, it downloads Google's emulator and system image from dl.google.com (before 0.6.4,
        it also checked dl.google.com when the device menu opened). Those sites see your IP address when they serve the
        download, as any website does.
    </p>
    <p>
        Each time it opens, the Android phone app asks our server at updates.sikemux.com whether a newer version of its
        own code is available, and downloads it from there. Every update is signed, and the phone checks the signature
        before using it. With each check the app sends its platform, an ID for the app build it is, and its update
        channel, so the server can pick the right update. Our server logs these requests like any other, as described
        above.
    </p>

    <h2>Coding agents</h2>
    <p>
        Sikemux runs agents such as Claude Code and Codex on your computer. Each agent talks to its own company under its
        own terms and privacy policy, and Sikemux does not send what you or your agents write to us.
    </p>

    <h2>Who handles data for us</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Clerk</strong> runs sign-in and stores your account details.</li>
        <li><strong>Google and GitHub</strong> confirm who you are when you choose to sign in with them.</li>
        <li>
            <strong>Cloudflare</strong> checks that a new account is being made by a person, not a bot, and stores our
            encrypted backups of the account database.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hostinger</strong> hosts the server that keeps your device list, serves phone app updates, and runs
            our relay, which passes encrypted connections between your devices when they cannot reach each other
            directly.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Apple Push Notification service and Google Firebase Cloud Messaging</strong> deliver notifications
            to your phone.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>n0</strong>, the makers of the networking library Sikemux uses, run the relay servers that releases
            before 0.5 use instead of ours. With remote access on, those releases also list their public key and relay
            with n0's lookup service, so your devices can find them.
        </li>
        <li><strong>PostHog</strong>, in the EU, counts the usage statistics and website visits described above.</li>
        <li><strong>Sentry</strong>, in the EU, receives the crash reports, sessions and traces described above.</li>
        <li><strong>Vercel</strong> hosts this website, and <strong>GitHub</strong> hosts the downloads.</li>
        <li>
            <strong>Paddle</strong> takes the payment for a paid plan as the merchant of record. You give it your email
            address, your country and your payment details when you pay. We never receive your card number. We keep
            what you bought, what was charged and when.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Keeping and deleting your data</h2>
    <p>
        We keep your account and device list for as long as you have an account. To delete your account, email <a href="mailto:contact@nodelike.com">contact@nodelike.com</a> from the address on it. We delete the account
        and its devices within 30 days and confirm when it is done. The steps are on <a href="/delete-account">Delete your account</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
        We back up the account database so it can be restored if something goes wrong. Backups are kept for 30 days and
        then deleted, so a deleted account is gone from them 30 days after it is erased.
    </p>
    <p>
        You can also ask for a copy of what we hold about you, or for anything wrong to be corrected.
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>Sikemux is not meant for children under 13, and we do not knowingly keep accounts for them.</p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
        If this policy changes, the date at the top changes with it. If a change affects what we collect, we will say so
        in the app or on this website before it takes effect.
    </p>
